From 4d6e8f0a2b4c6d8e0f1a3b5c7d9e1f2a4b6c8d0e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: m5agent <m5agent@m5stack.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 03:55:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] hw/m5stopwatch: fix BMI270 status/temp fixtures for batch-10 evidence

Fix two model-fixture gaps found by batch-10 guest/Device Service evidence on
the canonical 33-layer chain through 0152. The patch adds no BMI270 blob
execution or physical motion model.

- The frozen generic BMI270 driver loads CONFIG_DATA and then polls
  INTERNAL_STATUS bit0. The model intentionally does not execute the blob, but
  it did not set the init-complete status bit, so every guest initialization
  timed out. Expose 0x21=0x01 as an init-status fixture after the accepted
  burst.
- The driver reads TEMP at 0x22 as a little-endian int16. The batch-7 model
  wrote temperature at 0x20 (big-endian) only, so the guest read a stale/zero
  raw value. Alias the same synthetic temperature at 0x22 little-endian while
  keeping the existing 0x20 layout for the batch-7 mini-imu-pro path.

Result: the generic BMI270 driver initializes and reads the modeled accel/gyro/
temperature fixture subset for dof6/dof9/dof10. BMM350 0x14 and SPA06 0x76 are
still not modeled; the guest values carry the driver's own scale factors and
the 23C offset, so they are deterministic fixture readbacks, not physical units.
--- a/hw/m5stopwatch/m5_unit_i2c_sensors.c
+++ b/hw/m5stopwatch/m5_unit_i2c_sensors.c
@@ -1404,6 +1404,16 @@
     }
     int16_t temp = (int16_t)CLAMP(llround((s->values[9] - 25.0) * 512.0), -32768, 32767);
     be16(r + 0x20, (uint16_t)temp);
+    /* The frozen BMI270 driver reads TEMP at 0x22 (not 0x20); expose the same
+     * fixture in both slots so the batch-7 mini-imu-pro layout and the generic
+     * BMI270 driver both see the synthetic temperature. */
+    le16(r + 0x22, (uint16_t)temp);  /* driver reads TEMP as little-endian */
+    /* U228/U229/U230 batch-10 guest evidence: the frozen BMI270 driver loads
+     * CONFIG_DATA and polls INTERNAL_STATUS bit0 after the init burst. The
+     * model intentionally does not execute the blob, but it must report the
+     * init-complete status bit after the accepted burst or every guest init
+     * times out. This is a status fixture, not proof of blob execution. */
+    r[0x21] = 0x01;
 }
 
 static void mpu_accel_range_set(Object *obj, Visitor *v, const char *name,
